### Manual Imperative Retry Logic in TypeScript (Anti-Pattern)

Source: https://github.com/pauljphilp/effectpatterns/blob/main/content/published/control-repetition-with-schedule.mdx

Illustrates an anti-pattern by showing manual, stateful, and complex retry logic using recursion and `Effect.sleep`. This approach is contrasted with the declarative `Schedule` primitive. The function `manualRetry` takes an effect, remaining retries, and a delay, attempting to retry upon encountering an 'ApiError'. It is verbose and hard to compose.

```typescript
import { Effect } from "effect"
// Assuming flakyEffect is imported from somewhere
// import { flakyEffect } from "./somewhere"

// Mock flakyEffect for demonstration
const flakyEffect = Effect.fail("ApiError" as const);

// ❌ WRONG: Manual, stateful, and complex retry logic.
function manualRetry(
  effect: Effect.Effect<string, "ApiError">,
  retriesLeft: number,
  delay: number,
): Effect.Effect<string, "ApiError"> {
  return effect.pipe(
    Effect.catchTag("ApiError", () => {
      if (retriesLeft > 0) {
        return Effect.sleep(delay).pipe(
          Effect.flatMap(() => manualRetry(effect, retriesLeft - 1, delay * 2)),
        );
      }
      return Effect.fail("ApiError" as const);
    }),
  );
}

const program = manualRetry(flakyEffect, 5, 100);
console.log(program); // This would execute the manual retry logic if run
```

